WOW-BG3 benilla patch 0031: WoW's sound reaches the desktop reliably.
On Linux the output is a PulseAudio stream to the desktop's sound server (PipeWire's pipewire-pulse
or PulseAudio; libpulse loaded at runtime, no new crate) instead of cpal's ALSA, which on PipeWire
is pipewire-alsa: its per-cycle copy ran inside benilla's own nice-5, non-realtime unit from a
23 ms ring at 44.1 kHz, dropped 11 ms about every second even idle (209 PipeWire xruns in 2 min on
the desktop), and under load wedged cpal on the POLLERR (0030 rebuilt the stream each time).
The server now holds an 85 ms queue for WoW and runs the per-cycle work in its own realtime thread;
48 kHz float stereo (no resampling), it follows the default output, server underflows are counted
and reported, a lost server reconnects. The device queue counts toward the 100 ms mix-ahead.
cpal's ALSA stays as the fallback (no server socket, or WOW_AUDIO_OUTPUT=alsa). World mode logs
when WoW's output mutes or unmutes with BG3's own sound.
